# The Almanac of Boon.land — creatures, antiques, artifacts, contrivances, and bracework
# Worldbuilding canon for the 2026-11 chapter pass. Nouns and adjectives the land keeps.
# Every entry must DO something in a scene (cost, help, misread, remember) — no catalogue dumps.
# Lowercase compound nouns by preference; proper names are rare and earned.
#>almanac
#:surface #!canon #!worldbuilding
#:status #!working

^"almanac"{
  .status: "working"
  purpose: "Give every region a texture of its own: creature wonder where the land is wild, inventive contrivances where people build, and bracework (Spw as an in-world craft) where makers combine things into small realms."
  &boonhonk: ~".spw/story-boonhonk.spw#lore_land_story_boonhonk"
  &book_two: ~".spw/surfaces/wonder-about-pie.spw#book_two"
  rule: "The world believes its impossible premises completely. A wonder has a habit, a cost, and someone who misreads it."
  data: "book/content/world/almanac.json (83 collectible wonders: id, name, kind, region, terms, note, hint, home, echoes; a creature's motion and misread, an antique's provenance, an artifact's residue and made_by)"
  kinds: "book/scripts/modules/kinds.mjs — the one registry every feature reads (sigil, shelf, ink token, motion in the lanes, how it is found, art fields, badge); how the kinds live across the site: .spw/surfaces/ecology.spw"
  runtime: "chapter-render.mjs marks the first naming of each wonder per chapter (.wonder-mark); almanac.mjs collects on tap (lore.almanac.v1); /almanac/ shows the collection and the combining bench; desk ?[almanac]; switch wonders"
  revisit: "Every wonder has a home chapter and at least one echo; a reader's entry fills in place by place."
}

^"kept_lines"{
  .status: "trial (switch kept-lines), 2026-11-13"
  module: "book/scripts/modules/kept-lines.mjs (pure, ts-checked); shown by /almanac/ (almanac-page.mjs) and the chapter wonder card (almanac.mjs)"
  thing_line: "A thing's likeness (sigil, braces, material, valence, who keeps it, the chapters it lives in) read as Spw, so the seal and the line are two readings of one thing: @torch_ants{ $steam =boon &ants ~burrows .lives[1 2 8 12 14] }. Still to find, the probe stands where the name will be and nobody who keeps it is named: ?creature{ $steam =boon ~burrows .lives[1 2 8 12 14] }. Kept, the kind's own sigil answers it."
  shelf_line: "The whole almanac as one sealed line, each shelf under its sigil in the book's order, ?n for each run still to find: *shelf{_shelf @creatures[torch_ants ?3 salt_whales ?19] … .day[…] flehs_}. It shows where the gaps fall and nothing of what fills them. The parser says a hole as wonder 3."
  chapter_line: "On a chapter's wonder card: *chapter[1]{ @torch_ants ?7 }, what is kept here and the shape of what is not."
  given_line: "A line pasted, or carried in an address (?shelf=), is read beside the reader's own: what both keep, theirs by hint and not by name (the line itself carries its words), the reader's own by name. It is never written to lore.almanac.v1, and it leaves the address once read. A letter, not a key."
  bench: "The bench knows a combine the book writes (a bracework's terms; & either way round): {frost & hearth} is Hearth-drift, <river ~ moon> is Moonrun. It opens the door to where it is written and keeps nothing."
  unsaid: "No line names the figure in plain lines (no &figure); the Egg's card and the figure have no line at all."
  storage: "No new key: every line is read from lore.almanac.v1 and likeness.json."
  check: "npm run smoke, the two kept lines scenarios. A round trip of all 83 wonders, kept and not, through parse, spokenLine and scanPairLabels was run once by hand (2026-11-13) and is in no check yet."
}

?open[
  "Should the bench keep a bracework a reader writes before reading it? Proposed: no; it opens the door to where the book writes it, though kinds.mjs says bracework is combined on the bench"
  "Should a given shelf line be kept as a letter beside the reader's own, or only read?"
  "Should the seal itself be drawn from the line (the visual-wonder draft, proposal 3), so the two readings become one drawing?"
]

^"registers"{
  creature_wonder: "the dawnward field, the grove, the salt flats, the Watering Eye, the bloom garden, the confluence, the kept Thursday"
  contrivance: "Bonk City, Council Hill, the Quiet Rift hall, Bone.land's great frame, the Round Archive, the parcel gate"
  bracework: "wherever makers build or record: the council slab, the glaziers' panes, the joiners' mortises, the archive catalogue, the night school"
}

^"bestiary"{
  torch_ants: "keep lit streets along the berry roots all winter; businesslike, particular about torches; the warmth under the field is theirs"
  echo_wrens: "small brown wrens of the grove that sing a beat late; nest in hollow cedars; a wren-call is the grove saying again"
  lag_stag: "a white stag of the grove nobody has seen; its hoofprints press into the moss a beat after it has passed"
  rehearsal_moths: "dusk moths whose wings carry tomorrow's weather, a little smudged; the grove's almanac"
  salt_whales: "vast slow creatures under the salt flats; the flats are the roof of their road; old whale-roads glow under the crust, and two of them want the same turn"
  signal_skinks: "finger-long lizards that bask on the signal stones and flick their tails in a code only the stones answer"
  weathercocks: "iron roosters of Council Hill that vote by turning; the crosswind is their disagreement made weather"
  hum_crickets: "live in the Quiet Rift's crack and keep its pitch; go silent when someone lies near the floor"
  pane_snails: "glass-snails of Bonk City that graze soot off the panes at night; their slow trails are how a pane is forgiven clear"
  oath_moths: "grey market moths drawn to sworn words; they settle in the oath groove only when a promise is true"
  remembrancers: "pale fish in the Watering Eye that swim through the faces the well has kept, folding them gently so they fit"
  jazz_geese: "geese that fly in nines and honk in uneven time; one of them taught the Fool his note, or the other way round"
  chimney_swifts: "roost in the ridge chimneys and carry a sound a little farther than it meant to go"
  load_beetles: "small dark beetles in Bone.land timber that click when a beam is asked to bear more than it agreed to; a lit-street beetle in chapter 12 is one of their cousins"
  crown_wasps: "build paper crowns in the bloom garden, beautiful and hollow; a rented crown is wasp-work"
  early_bees: "arrive at a flower before it opens and wait, humming, as if the bloom owed them"
  seam_otters: "swim exactly on the unblended seam of the two rivers, one paw in each water"
  ink_herons: "stand in the berry-dark river so still their reflections dry into the bank"
  shelf_owls: "keep the gaps in the Round Archive's shelves clear for chronicles not yet written; they nest in what is missing"
  hob_deer: "deer no taller than a boot that graze between frost-ruts only on days nobody keeps; an unkept creature"
  page_fox: "a fox the colour of an unturned page, seen only on unkept days, trotting as if late for something"
  gate_pigeons: "sleep on the parcel gate's counterweight and ride it up and down all night without waking"
  frill_lichen: "grows round cut letters on any stone read aloud: a ruffle with more edge than room, folding and folding and never running out of edge (Book Two's undercurrent: hyperbolic, like a crocheted reef)"
  remainder_moths: "fly round a new flame in laps that never close, each a little past the last, so none lands on the same spot twice (the undercurrent: a turn by an amount that never comes out even, like π)"
}

^"contrivances"{
  press_stones: "the archivist's flat stones for holding a map's crease; one is always missing"
  drying_line: "where the map-house hangs new maps to dry; maps that refuse ink drip back into the trough"
  want_needle: "the Genie's brass needle, quick to find a want; circles, slows, tries again"
  resolution_glass: "the Genie's long glass; it shows only wants larger than a loaf"
  wish_scale: "the Genie's pan scale, balanced against coincidences"
  bottle_desk: "the folding desk the Genie keeps in his bottle, with a drawer for Thursdays"
  key_file_bench: "Council Hill's bench where keys are filed smaller, never grown; the filings are swept into a jar called the honest jar"
  crosswind_loom: "weaves the hill's two winds into the council's tablecloth; when the vote turns, the weave changes colour"
  crack_gauge: "brass calipers set across the Rift's crack; they measure in notes, not inches"
  bonk_kiln: "the forgiving kiln of Bonk City; melts down old impacts into clear glass"
  dent_lanterns: "lanterns hammered from dented brass; each dent is a street the light remembers"
  chime_gutters: "gutters that ring when it rains, so the city can hear which roofs are leaking"
  lamp_ladders: "the lamplighters' hinged ladders that fold into a staff"
  folio_press: "glasses a page flat and warm, layering it over the last"
  oath_scale: "weighs the fruit an oath is paid in; ignores the hand that pays"
  well_winch: "the Watering Eye's winch; on still nights the bucket comes up holding a face instead of water"
  shard_rack: "the Fool's rack for the nine honk shards; one slot egg-shaped"
  listening_mallet: "BoneWAP's felt-headed mallet for asking a beam what it will bear"
  load_bells: "small bells hung from Bone.land's joists that ring when weight passes over"
  rail_pins: "brass pins on the confluence bridge where receipts are posted for any traveller to read"
  ferry_bell: "rung at the confluence when someone needs to cross the seam without wetting both springs"
  glassing_press: "the Round Archive's great press for folios; it pressed every page but the last"
  storm_weight: "the parcel gate's iron counterweight; it lets one man lift a gate meant for three"
  door_rule: "the carpenter's folding rule; it has a hinge for every size of door"
  pie_dividers: "brass compasses the night school uses to divide paper pies evenly; they never come out even"
  brace_boxes: "small glass boxes with sigil-etched lids, sold in Bonk City; each holds a pocket realm combined from two nouns and a sigil"
  sliver_tin: "the night school's tin for the slivers the pie-dividers leave over; each winter's fill half the room the last left, so it is always filling and never full (the undercurrent: halves that sum toward one and never arrive)"
}

^"antiques"{
  .note: "Old made things the land keeps, moved out of the contrivances (2026_11_09.F): each has a provenance — who made it, who keeps it, what it remembers. A contrivance is used; an antique is kept. In the lanes it stays, waiting longest by its lane, and keeping it steadies the pie the way old things do."
  members: "press_stones, bonk_kiln, dent_lanterns, chime_gutters, oath_scale, well_winch, shard_rack, load_bells, glassing_press, storm_weight, crosswind_loom, ferry_bell"
  rule: "Its provenance is a small history, never a plot: it may name a maker, a keeper and a memory, and a chapter it was made or kept in; it never explains the Egg or names the figure."
}

^"artifacts"{
  .note: "What a scene leaves behind, marked by what happened in it (story-boonhonk.spw#residue_after_departure: a remainder the next scene can inherit), and what it asks of whoever picks it up. Some are found in the chapters, named in the words of the scene that left them; some are made in the lanes, by what a carry does, and come to the almanac on their own."
  found: "horn_button (Fifteen, lost in Six), waxed_slice (Six, Ten, Fourteen), gate_book_entry and damage_slip (Fifteen), relit_torch and criers_note (Fourteen), bridge_receipts (Twelve, Fifteen)"
  made_in_the_lanes: "stacked_plate (the first WAP of any carry), found_line (five words kept from the road)"
  rule: "Terms are the scene's own phrase, not a common word (a torch that had gone out, both receipts, saw a hat), so an artifact is marked where it was left and nowhere it merely shares a noun."
}

^"bracework"{
  .note: "Spw as an in-world low craft (the bone register). Makers write what they mean to build in sigils and braces before they build it, because a thing written can be checked, combined, and refused before anyone carries a beam."
  why: "Braces say what is held together; sigils say how. Written first, a plan can be leaned on (Bone.land), read by strangers (the rail pins), or combined into something nobody planned (brace-boxes)."
  containers: {
    frame: "[] a window — you can only look into it"
    body: "{} a room — you can stand in it for a minute"
    scope: "() a gathering — everything in it must arrive together"
    channel: "<> a passage — you can go through it, never stay"
  }
  sigils_in_use: {
    confluence: "& merges two things without whitening either (the council slab, the rivers)"
    potential: "~ lets one thing dream of being the other (mist, rehearsal)"
    perspective: "@ lets one thing watch the other, which changes both a little"
    integration: "^ lifts one thing by the other (ridge-beams, rising processions)"
    annotation: "# the face that travels (the street face of a pane)"
    ground: ". the face that stays (the inside face of a pane, the footing of a frame)"
  }
  combines: "The night school's game: draw two nouns from a hat, choose a sigil and a container, close the braces, and the brace-box fogs into a small weather. {frost & hearth} snows warm. <river ~ moon> is a passage where water is thinking about being light. [grove @ dog] is a window where the trees watch back. (ant ^ lantern) is a procession that rises."
  limit: "A brace-box holds only what both nouns agree on, and it runs on borrowed warmth: it goes cold in a week unless someone holds it. The ants disapprove of brace-boxes; they know where the warmth comes from."
  guard: "Bracework stays a craft people use, not a spell that solves the plot. Never decode WAPboy or the Egg in it."
}

^"adjectives"{
  .note: "A palette, not a quota. Compound adjectives built from the land's own materials; use them where a plain word would blur."
  palette: "loam-dark, frost-bitten, honey-slow, chalk-pale, berry-dark, ember-warm, lamp-lit, glass-clear, rain-slick, salt-stiff, bell-bright, moth-soft, crumb-sweet, iron-cold, smoke-blue, tallow-yellow, jam-thick, burr-rough, owl-quiet, wren-quick, kiln-warm, dent-bright, soot-furred, moss-thick, sap-slow, needle-fine, lantern-ripe, ledger-grey, felt-headed, half-forgiven, beat-late, square-cornered, unkept, thrice-folded, crust-gold, well-black, chime-loud, joist-honest, hollow-proud, two-sprung"
}

^"placements"{
  .note: "Which chapter introduces what (first sighting); later chapters may echo."
  c01: "torch_ants (deepen), press_stones, drying_line; a first bracework glimpse: the archivist's margin sigils"
  c02: "echo_wrens, lag_stag (prints only), rehearsal_moths"
  c03: "salt_whales, signal_skinks, want_needle, resolution_glass, wish_scale, bottle_desk"
  c04: "weathercocks, key_file_bench, crosswind_loom; bracework on the council slab: &[boon, bane]{table}"
  c05: "hum_crickets, crack_gauge"
  c06: "pane_snails, bonk_kiln, dent_lanterns, chime_gutters, lamp_ladders, folio_press, brace_boxes (a stall); glaziers' # and . faces"
  c07: "oath_moths, oath_scale"
  c08: "remembrancers, well_winch"
  c09: "jazz_geese, chimney_swifts, shard_rack; the nine shards as a combine"
  c10: "load_beetles, listening_mallet, load_bells; mortise bracework (^ at the ridge, . at the footing)"
  c11: "crown_wasps, early_bees"
  c12: "seam_otters, ink_herons, rail_pins, ferry_bell"
  c13: "shelf_owls, glassing_press; the brace-catalogue (the blank last line is an open brace)"
  c14: "hob_deer, page_fox (unkept creatures of the missing Thursday); torch_ants echo"
  c15: "gate_pigeons, storm_weight, door_rule, pie_dividers; the night school's combines; the clerk's new form [child, parcel, door]"
}
